{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YZsRvoFpW8AHHADN9dtCCg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, so GNU ls `-l1` → long format, even when piped. So `-l` always beats `-1` regardless of order in GNU ls.\n\nBut looking at the GNU coreutils source code, `-1` and `-l` both set the `format` variable, so last should win. Unless there's something else going on...\n\nLet me check the GNU coreutils source. In `ls.c`:\n\n```c\ncase '1':\n  /* -1 has no effect after -l.  */\n  if (format != long_format)\n    format = one_per_line;\n  break;\n```\n\nThere it is! `-1` explicitly checks if format is already `long_format` and does NOT override it. \n\nSo `-l` dominates `-1`, and the comment even says so. I need to fix our implementation: `-1` should NOT override `-l`. Looking at the original code, it already had this check with `if !opts.longFormat` preventing `-1` from overriding `-l`, but my change broke that by making `-1` unconditionally reset `longFormat`. Let me revert that and restore the proper precedence where `-l` takes priority.\n\nThe fix is to check if long format is already set before applying the single column mode, while still clearing the column and sort flags. Now I'm working through the interaction between these flags more carefully—specifically what happens with a sequence like `-1lC1` where the flags get applied in order and later ones can override earlier ones, except `-1` shouldn't override `-l`.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1437,"cache_read_input_tokens":40854,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1437},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f653e7dc-b04b-4570-8726-46e6cbeb1492","uuid":"9b54d524-e277-46d6-986f-83803c76